  "problem": "A certain company produces two types of smartphones: Phone $A$ and Phone $B.$ The following table describes how the number of customers changes between both types of smartphones each year:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" align=\"center\">From:</td> \n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td width=\"80\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Phone $A$</b></td>\n<td width=\"80\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Phone $B$</b></td> \n</tr>\n<tr> \n<td rowspan=\"2\" align=\"center\">To:</td>\n<td width=\"80\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Phone $A$</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr> \n<td width=\"80\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Phone $B$</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nThat is, $40%$ of Phone $A$ customers switch to Phone $B$ each year, and $30%$ of Phone $B$ customers switch to Phone $A.$\n\nCurrently, $600\\,000$ customers use Phone $A,$ while $400\\,000$ customers use Phone $B.$ How many customers will use Phone $A$ in one year? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change.",
